# SigilGuard Cheatsheet

## Gate Verdicts

SigilGuard gates return `%SigilGuard.Decision{}`. Treat `action` as the 1.0
verdict.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.ToolGateway

context = [
  phase: :tool_request,
  origin: :model,
  sink: :tool,
  tool: "repo_file_write",
  actor: "spiffe://example.test/agents/release-bot",
  trust_zone: :trusted
]

decision = ToolGateway.guard_request(request, context)

case decision.action do
  :allow -> execute_tool(request)
  :redact -> execute_tool(decision.sanitized_text || request)
  :confirm -> request_human_approval(decision)
  :quarantine -> quarantine_request(decision)
  :block -> reject_request(decision)
end
```

Use `SigilGuard.ToolGateway` as the enforcement core. Use
`SigilGuard.MCP.Gateway` only at MCP transport boundaries where
JSON-RPC-compatible helper names and tuple responses are useful.

Common meanings:

- `:allow` — pass the payload through unchanged.
- `:redact` — use the sanitized payload or text from the decision.
- `:confirm` — require an action-bound confirmation token before execution.
- `:quarantine` — isolate the payload and surface evidence to the operator.
- `:block` — deny the operation.

For tool results, flip the boundary direction:

```elixir
result_context = [
  phase: :tool_result,
  origin: :tool,
  sink: :model,
  tool: "repo_file_write",
  actor: "spiffe://example.test/agents/release-bot"
]

decision = ToolGateway.guard_result(result, result_context)
```

## Policy Grammar

Repository policy files are loaded from the <code>SIGILGUARD</code> filename
family:

- `SIGILGUARD_POLICY`
- `.sigilguard-policy`
- `.sigilguard/policy`
- `.github/sigilguard-policy`

Legacy <code>SIGIL</code> filenames fail closed.

Minimal boundary-policy file:

```text
version 3

[rules]
default allow
block phase:tool_request zone:untrusted
confirm sensitivity:private origin:tool,resource,repo sink:external,network
allow phase:tool_result isolation:container,vm,remote_attested effect:read

[repo]
default require_approval
allow agent:release-bot action:modify docs/** CHANGELOG.md
block agent:* priv/secrets/**

[contracts]
contract sink:external,network max_size:16384
  no_raw_credentials:true digest_only_pii:true classes:text
  credential_transform:hash
```

Boundary-rule decisions are `allow`, `redact`, `confirm`, `quarantine`, or
`block`. Matchers are AND-ed by field and comma-separated within a field. Common
matchers include `phase`, `origin`, `sink`, `zone`, `trust`, `sensitivity`,
`isolation`, `effect`, `hits`, and `indicator`.

Repo-policy decisions inside `[repo]` are `allow`, `require_approval`, or
`block`; `require_approval` maps to the 1.0 `:confirm` gate verdict.

Load and evaluate the repo section directly when you only need repo path rules:

```elixir
{:ok, policy} = SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.load(repo_root)

decision =
  SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.evaluate(policy,
    phase: :tool_request,
    source: :model,
    sink: :tool,
    action: :write,
    tool: "repo_file_write",
    actor: "spiffe://example.test/agents/release-bot"
  )
```

Boundary policy can be evaluated directly when the host already has a normalized
boundary:

```elixir
decision =
  SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.evaluate(
    phase: :tool_request,
    source: :model,
    sink: :tool,
    action: :execute,
    trust_zone: :trusted,
    actor: "spiffe://example.test/agents/release-bot"
  )
```

## Attestation Sign And Verify

Create a statement from a decision, sign it with a host signer, and attach it
as `_agent_trust`.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.Attestation

{:ok, statement} =
  Attestation.from_decision(decision, context,
    payload: request,
    statement_type: :tool_request
  )

{:ok, envelope} =
  Attestation.sign(statement, MyApp.AgentSigner,
    keyid: "sha256:65b60673d6ed884bf01c2c222d82ada0740f29ac3355d6a925c81f17f47a27b8"
  )

request = Attestation.attach(request, envelope)
```

Verify an attached attestation with direct trust material or keys derived from a
verified trust bundle.

```elixir
trust_material = %{
  "sha256:65b60673d6ed884bf01c2c222d82ada0740f29ac3355d6a925c81f17f47a27b8" =>
    public_key_b64u
}

with {:ok, envelope} <- Attestation.fetch(request),
     {:ok, statement} <-
       Attestation.verify(envelope, trust_material,
         payload: request,
         context: context,
         consume: true
       ) do
  {:ok, statement}
end
```

## Confirmation Flow

Confirmation is bound to the exact payload and boundary context. If either
changes, verification fails.

```elixir
secret_key = System.fetch_env!("SIGILGUARD_CONFIRMATION_KEY")

decision = SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_request(request, context)

{:ok, token} =
  SigilGuard.ToolGateway.issue_confirmation(
    request,
    context,
    decision,
    secret_key,
    ttl_ms: 120_000
  )

confirmed_request = SigilGuard.Attestation.attach_confirmation(request, token)

case SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_confirmed_request(
       confirmed_request,
       context,
       confirmation_key: secret_key
     ) do
  %SigilGuard.Decision{action: :allow} -> execute_tool(confirmed_request)
  %SigilGuard.Decision{} = denied -> reject_request(denied)
end
```

Use the out-of-band option when the host keeps tokens outside the MCP payload:

```elixir
SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_confirmed_request(
  request,
  context,
  confirmation_key: secret_key,
  confirmation_token: token
)
```

The same pattern applies to result confirmation with
`guard_confirmed_result/3`.
